.\"
.\" UMOUNT(2) — Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH UMOUNT 2 "May 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
umount, umount2, unmount \- detach a mounted filesystem
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RB ( libc ", " -lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/mount.h>
.PP
.BI "int umount(const char *" target );
.BI "int umount2(const char *" target ", int " flags );
.BI "int unmount(const char *" target ", int " flags );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR umount ()
detaches the filesystem mounted at
.IR target ;
it is equivalent to
.BR umount2 ( target ", 0)."
.PP
.BR umount2 ()
takes an additional
.I flags
argument:
.TP
.B MNT_FORCE
Drop the filesystem even if it has open files or active vnodes.
Stale file descriptors against the filesystem will start failing
with
.B EIO
once the unmount completes.  Use only when a graceful unmount is
known to be impossible (a hung NFS mount, a removed device).
.TP
.B MNT_UPDATE
Reserved.  Currently no effect for unmount.
.PP
.BR unmount ()
is the BSD spelling of
.BR umount2 ();
both refer to the same syscall.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, 0 is returned.  On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller's effective UID is not 0.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I flags
contains an unknown bit, or
.I target
is malformed.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.I target
isn't currently a mount point.
.TP
.B EBUSY
The filesystem still has open files and
.B MNT_FORCE
was not set.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I target
points outside the calling process's address space.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
umount:   syscall number 22
umount2:  syscall number 161
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (2),
.BR umount (8)
